Conclusion
In this lab, students built a complete rollback workflow for a containerized application, starting with a simple deploy script that validates each new version with a health check before accepting it, and a rollback script that automatically reverts to the last successful version when a deployment fails. By wiring these into a pipeline script, students observed a failed deployment trigger an automatic rollback end to end, verified through both log output and live application responses. Extending this into a blue-green deployment model showed how maintaining two parallel environments and only switching traffic after a health check passes allows rollback to become a near-instant pointer change rather than a disruptive container replacement. These patterns, health-gated deployment, automatic rollback on failure, and traffic-switching for zero downtime, are directly applicable to real-world CI/CD systems and form a foundation for safely deploying software changes.
